A||||10||Gokart||Sealer for plenum?||||5||
Z||000000||Gokart||11-27-1999||09:58 PM||||I'm removing my upper manifold plenum tomorrow and putting the one on from my parts car so I can repaint my original one. I have the gaskets, but no manual(Haynes nor Chiltons)has instructions on replacing! Will the gaskets be good enough or should I use RTV type sealant as well? TIA for advice.||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||11-27-1999||11:14 PM||||I do not ever recomend RTV on intake parts. Ever. If pieces come lose it can hang a valve open and burn it.<P>If you feel you want a sealer use one of these three. None of these will extrude into the intake and leave dangerous pieces. All will provide excelent sealing and retention. Follow lable directions.<P>Permatex High Tack, available as spray or paint.<BR>Permatex Super 300, available as paint.<BR>Balcamp or other "aviation" sealer, available as paint.<P>Paint sealers come in the tradtional "ruber cement" can/jar with brush.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Linx||11-28-1999||06:10 PM||||go with the gaskets; the rtv can be a pain in the butt to remove, in comparison to just popping off the gaskets.  There isnt alot of pressure here, and the gasket doesnt get fowled by exposure to oil or gas, so they pop out rather easily and can be used again.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||11-29-1999||12:57 AM||||RTV will wipe out the O2 sensor. Ultra Blue is fine (o2 sensor safe).<P>However, gorilla snot is not needed on the intake except for the lower manifold to block in the front and back. The plenum gaskets are all that is needed.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||11-29-1999||12:19 PM||||I agree with Terryk,<P>I just finished with my rebuild and I used the dry gaskets between the intake plenum parts.  I used the black RTV on the intake manifold at either end.  No problems and wngine runs great (especially after I found the PCV vacuum hose disconnected from the underside of the upper plenum , oops).||||reg||1
Z||000005||dogtired||11-29-1999||05:05 PM||||Just some thoughts here for all that have done it?<BR>When I replaced the plenum on the 86GT I put a (very) light coat of oil on it. Seemed to work great for a couple weeks now I have a vacuum leak, since I checked all hoses and fittings was thinking it may be the plenum gaskets, and was toying with the idea of removing it again and sealing it to both sides. Would that be a dumb move? Should I be looking other places for my leak? Vacuum leaks really SUCK  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000006||theogre||11-29-1999||09:50 PM||||All RTV used in Fiero should be the sensor of the safe kinds only. I forget just what's in the regular kind right now that kill the O2 sensor. You can get away with regular on water jacket parts, it's a toss up on oil pan and valve covers.<P>I hadn't had a set of v6 intakes apart. that's why I said if you feel you need sealant.....<P>Most people abuse RTV. That's why I said never use it on intake work. The others I listed above won't make snot globs.||||reg||1
Z||000007||pollock||11-30-1999||05:43 PM||||Are the gaskets for the upper intake readily available?  Where?||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||11-30-1999||11:14 PM||||The dealer.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Linx||12-01-1999||01:27 AM||||Autozone.  Theyve got a gasket set that has it all; upper, intermediate and lower gaskets, upper coolent gasket, egr tube gaskets and I cant remember what else.  for about $15.  It's meant for several vehicles so you'll have leftovers, but they're all there.<p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 12-01-1999).]||||reg||3
Z||000010||Carrolles||12-01-1999||07:13 AM||||A great source of vacuum leaks is often due to the hard plastic vacuum tubing cracking.  This is hard to detect without actually seeing the crack.  I found several of mine brittle and cracked when I pulled the intake off.  The hard plastic tubing is no longer available from Pontiac (so I'm told).  I had to splice regular vavuum hose on a number of mine.||||reg||1
